Gap status:
- 4B.1 / 4B.2 / 4B.8: resolved more cleanly through sham referenda, annexation, and forced territorial absorption.
- 4B.3 / 4B.5 / 4B.6 / 4B.9: resolved for the education/propaganda and censorship side of the chapter.
- 4B.4: partly resolved, but direct evidence of independent domestic institutional restraint inside Russia remains thin.
- 4B.7: partly resolved, especially for occupied Crimea and annexed territories, but broader Russian-domestic political equality evidence is still thinner than repression evidence.
- 4B.10: resolved in direction, not in magnitude; the 2022 trajectory is clearly weaker and more coercive than the inherited baseline.

Blockers:
- One relevant UN/press result returned an internal browser error, so I used OSCE, Amnesty, USDOS, Freedom House, and HRW sources that opened cleanly.
- I did not find a new high-value source on genuinely competitive Russian elections in 2022; the strongest additions were instead on sham referenda, annexation, compulsory citizenship, and propaganda/surveillance.

Wave 1 ledger: about 40 candidates screened, 18 documents opened, 10 source-claim units accepted, with several direct-fetch blockers on `government.ru` and some `rosstat.gov.ru` pages (timeouts / 502s). I used accessible Reuters, IMF, Interfax, RBC, CBR, ConsultantPlus, Euronews, and Freedom House pages to avoid repeating the same story.

